    terms: [["Integrity", "Moral wholeness; words and deeds in harmony."], ["Hypocrisy", "Pretending to be what one is not."], ["Restitution", "Making good what was damaged or taken."]],
    intro: "We have reached the end of the course. We began with the God of truth and defined integrity as wholeness before him. We saw how dishonesty arises and how disciples speak plainly and truthfully in love, keep promises, work honestly, handle money without bribery, practise honesty as students and citizens, and keep private life consistent with public. We studied hypocrisy, self-deception, honesty with God, pressure, courage, relationships, leaders, digital honesty, repair after failure, reputation and growth in character.",
    context: "This is the default week of the final examination, which covers all lessons. Churches may hold the examination during class or at another time, and where it is held elsewhere, this session works as a guided final review. The examination is built from the approved question bank following the curriculum blueprint, and results are released according to the church's settings.",
    observe: "The course has shown that integrity flows from God's truthful character and the grace of Christ, is practised in every sphere of life, is tested by pressure and secrecy, is restored after failure by repentance and restitution, and grows by the Spirit over a lifetime in community.",
    points: [
      ["God's truth and the nature of integrity", "God is truth; integrity is wholeness; dishonesty arises from fear, pride and greed. Recall Numbers 23:19, John 14:6, Psalm 15, Proverbs 10:9 and John 8:44. Ask learners to think of one example from daily life and share it with a neighbour. Invite learners to say how this truth could shape one decision this week."],
      ["Integrity in speech, promises, work and money", "Disciples speak plainly and lovingly, keep promises, work honestly, refuse bribes and are honest in study and citizenship. Recall Matthew 5:37, Ephesians 4:15, Psalm 15:4, Colossians 3:23, Exodus 23:8 and Luke 16:10. Ask learners to think of one example from daily life and share it with a neighbour."],
      ["Integrity in the heart, under pressure and over time", "Private life matches public, hypocrisy and self-deception are confronted, honesty with God and others is practised, pressure is met with courage, failure is repaired, and character grows. Recall Psalm 101:2, Luke 18:14, Psalm 139:23-24, Daniel 3:17-18, Proverbs 28:13 and 2 Peter 1:5-7."],
    ],
    discuss: [
      ["Which parts of this course have most changed how you speak, work or handle money?", "Ask each learner to name one lesson and one practical change, linking answers to the passages studied."],
      ["How would you explain biblical integrity to a young person facing pressure to cheat?", "Invite clear, gracious explanations with a verse and a practical step."],
      ["What question about honesty or character do you still carry?", "Write the questions down for pastoral follow-up and private conversation."],
      ["What step will you take to grow in integrity after this course?", "Press for a specific habit, a time and a person who will help you keep it."],
    ],
    apply: "Review your notes and the memory verses. Revise the main passages and your growth plan. Pray for peace before the examination and thank God for what you have learned. Plan one habit of integrity that you will continue after the course.",
    activity: "Run a quick oral review. The teacher reads a clue such as a false balance is an abomination and learners name the topic and passage. Ask each learner to name one habit they will keep. End with a prayer of commitment and a prayer for the examination.",
    takeHome: "Prepare for the examination by reviewing the revision map, the memory verses and your own summary. Rest well the night before. Pray for classmates.",
    prayer: "Thank God for each lesson. Ask for calm minds and clear memory in the examination. Pray that what we have learned will shape our character.",
    teacher: "Explain how the examination works: time allowed, question types, and when results will be released. Reassure anxious learners and ensure fair conditions for all, including those who need support. If the examination is held on another date, use this session for guided revision. Model integrity in the way the examination is run.",
    assess: "This unit is the final examination week. The final examination covers Lessons 1 to 24 and is built from the approved question bank following the curriculum blueprint.",
    mcq1: ["Which verse says to speak the truth with your neighbour?", ["Ephesians 4:25", "Genesis 1:1", "Psalm 23", "Acts 2"], 0, "Ephesians 4:25."],
    mcq2: ["Which verse says whoever walks in integrity walks securely?", ["Proverbs 10:9", "John 3:16", "Romans 3:23", "Hebrews 11:1"], 0, "Proverbs 10:9."],
    tf: ["The final examination covers lessons 1 to 24.", true, "It reviews the whole course."],
    sa: ["In a few sentences, explain what the Bible teaches about integrity and honesty and how they are lived out.", ["god", "truth", "integrity", "honest", "speech", "character"], "A good answer says integrity is wholeness rooted in God's truthful character, lived out in plain loving speech, kept promises, honest work and money, courage under pressure and private consistency, restored by repentance and grown by grace."],
